n": -3.54, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 5.94, "baseline_margin": -1.66, "analyst_adjustment": 3.16, "key_factors": ["Prediction markets price Andrews at ~99%, and Nov 4 headlines describe the race tilting toward Democrats", "Pre-election polling was essentially tied, averaging roughly even to slightly Republican", "Appointee Graham faces experience doubts and a heavy-spending challenger in an R-leaning state", "Expert ratings mostly Lean R, conflicting with market and headline signals"], "rationale": "Polls and most expert ratings pointed to a dead-heat or slight Republican edge, but prediction markets price Andrews near certain and election-night coverage describes the race tilting toward Democrats amid a broader Democratic wave. Weighing that late-breaking evidence against a tied polling average, the forecast lands just ahead for Andrews with wide uncertainty.", "watch": "Counting and any late-arriving precincts in the Upstate could still flip a margin this narrow.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["Incumbent Darline Graham (R) is running."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's Senate race pits appointed incumbent Darline Graham against Democrat Annie Andrews, who has dramatically out-raised the incumbent. The state leans Republican by about eight points, but polling through early November showed the two essentially tied, with averages ranging from even to a couple of points Republican. Most expert ratings called the race Lean R or Tossup, though a few outlets flagged a Tossup. Doubts about Graham's experience surfaced in late coverage, and Andrews' fundraising edge kept her competitive. A Libertarian and a Constitution Party candidate also appear on the ballot. The race sits in a favorable national environment for Democrats, and election-night coverage and prediction markets moved sharply toward Andrews even as pre-election surveys remained split. The result is a genuinely close contest with modest Democratic momentum.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem":